WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.650
Alright, when starting out with version control in a production pipeline, it can be a little

2
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:11.849
intimidating and, in order to help with understanding source control, we will begin with some

3
00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:14.210
core concepts.

4
00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.800
First up, what is source control?

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.760
While you might already be familiar with this concept, in a nutshell, source control

6
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:28.739
is a means of providing a backup or redundancy in project and file management on a production.

7
00:00:28.739 --> 00:00:34.399
It is the management of changes to your computer files and assets over time.

8
00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:39.780
It allows you to track versions or revisions of files and assets as they are changed and

9
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:46.189
most commonly it is referred to as revision control or version control.

10
00:00:46.189 --> 00:00:51.109
Several version control solutions are integrated into Unreal Engine through the use of plugins

11
00:00:51.109 --> 00:00:57.549
that should be active automatically such as Git SCM, Perforce, Subversion, and Plastic

12
00:00:57.549 --> 00:00:59.320
CSM.

13
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:04.459
You can even use ShotGrid Toolkit for version control, but that will need you to enable

14
00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:08.969
the plugin before it can be used.

15
00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:14.049
Now source control can be formal or informal, and software is the means by which your

16
00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:18.019
production formalizes that process.

17
00:01:18.019 --> 00:01:22.859
While solutions can vary depending on your production needs, the best solutions we recommend

18
00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:28.060
are using a system like Perforce, SVN, or Git.

19
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.459
Another solution that is good but not great is something that uses a central storage system

20
00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:36.060
like Box or OneDrive.

21
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.540
ShotGrid Toolkit falls somewhere between the OK and Good categories, but is still

22
00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.599
a viable solution.

23
00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:48.299
If you're just using a network drive or naming out files that are stored on a dedicated

24
00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:54.680
external hard drive, that's an OK solution but one that is risky for both your production

25
00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.500
and data.

26
00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:01.900
Finally, leaving everything up to chance, storing your files on your internal hard drive,

27
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:06.890
and just hoping for the best is universally considered a bad solution.

28
00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:12.289
But you only live once, so if you are dead set on the YOLO solution, go for it.

29
00:02:12.289 --> 00:02:14.340
What's the worst that could happen?

30
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.139
The answer is terrible things.

31
00:02:16.139 --> 00:02:17.659
Terrible terrible things.

32
00:02:17.659 --> 00:02:21.569
Please don't employ this as your version control solution.

33
00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:25.930
Alright, now that you're familiar with what source control is, you probably want

34
00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:29.530
to know why you should use a source control solution.

35
00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.770
Well, source control allows you to have options that may save you in the future from

36
00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:34.770
disaster.

37
00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:39.300
A moment ago I mentioned redundancy and backup.

38
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.659
If something goes wrong, and inevitably it will, you can recover previous versions

39
00:02:44.659 --> 00:02:48.060
of the work that you've done and save your production.

40
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:53.460
You can, through source control, merge changes, so if a team member updates an asset or

41
00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:58.300
there is a small tweak, like the change of a color on a part of the asset, you can merge

42
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:02.819
those changes and then go back to previous versions if a director or producer changes

43
00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:06.280
their mind after viewing the results.

44
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:11.780
Your teams can share and distribute work between different users, and even different studios,

45
00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.460
with partition streams for access.

46
00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:20.620
Source control helps your InfoSec team maintain data security in a centralized location.

47
00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:25.800
It allows productions or projects to rapidly scale working environments so that multiple

48
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.840
users can be added or removed as needed.

49
00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.449
And overall, it's going to give you a better performance solution.

50
00:03:35.449 --> 00:03:38.680
So where does source control fit into the production workflow?

51
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:43.199
Well, source control is the foundation of your collaborative workflow, and there are

52
00:03:43.199 --> 00:03:47.120
several options for version control solutions.

53
00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:52.719
Systems such as Git SCM, Perforce, which is the most common, and ShotGrid can also

54
00:03:52.719 --> 00:03:54.919
serve as a source control option.

55
00:03:54.919 --> 00:04:00.340
Additionally, if you are working in UEFN, the Unreal Revision Control is a built-in

56
00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:07.419
source control option available for UEFN art teams to collaborate more effectively.

57
00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:12.699
With that said, this course will focus on Perforce as our version control solution

58
00:04:12.699 --> 00:04:16.800
and the foundation of our collaborative production pipeline.

59
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.120
As we start out with Perforce as the version control solution, there are two other core

60
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:27.920
pillars of our collaborative workflow that will be explored and filled in a little bit later.

61
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.759
That's it for the core concept.

62
00:04:29.759 --> 00:04:34.279
In our next section, I'll walk you through the basic workflow of a version control environment.
